{# Fil d'Ariane — always visible on mobile viewports (below the site's lg
   breakpoint, 992px), and on desktop only when the site runs as an
   installed PWA (display-mode: standalone). Visibility is 100% CSS (see
   app.css's .breadcrumb-bar rule) — no server flag, no JS detection,
   since this is a navigation convenience, never a security boundary
   (SECURITY §3, same principle as menu visibility).

   The home icon is always hard-coded to "/", never sourced from config.
   Everything past it comes from the current route's optional `breadcrumb`
   declaration (Core\Http\Router::addRoute()'s 6th argument for core routes,
   module.json's `routes[].breadcrumb` for module routes) — a route that
   declares none simply stops the trail at the home icon. `parents` are
   ancestor labels naming a *menu* (Core\View\MenuBuilder's `label`, e.g.
   "Espace chefs" — core routes derive this from
   Core\View\MenuBuilder::labelFor() rather than repeating the string, so a
   label rename only ever needs changing in MenuBuilder::MENUS itself;
   module.json breadcrumbs still hardcode their own copy, since a JSON
   manifest has no PHP to call that from).

   A parent is never a link to a specific page anymore — most menu
   categories have no single landing page of their own, and even the ones
   that do would send every visitor to the same arbitrarily-chosen page
   regardless of what they actually want. Instead, clicking a parent OPENS
   that menu's own section (public/assets/js/breadcrumb.js): the desktop
   submenu bar, or the mobile offcanvas with the matching accordion section
   expanded — letting the visitor pick the sub-page themselves, same as
   using the nav directly. Only rendered as a button when the parent label
   actually matches one of the current `menus` (the same global the nav
   itself renders from); otherwise it stays plain text, exactly as before.
   `breadcrumb_current`, when the controller passes it, replaces the
   route's static `label` for a dynamic page title (a member's name, an
   article title...); the current page is never itself a link.

   The home page itself declares its own breadcrumb too (label "Accueil",
   no parents) so it ends the trail the same way every other page does —
   an active, non-link "Accueil" item after the icon — rather than the
   icon being the only thing rendered there. #}
<nav class="breadcrumb-bar border-bottom bg-light px-3 py-2" aria-label="Fil d'Ariane">
    <ol class="breadcrumb mb-0 small align-items-center">
        <li class="breadcrumb-item">
            <a href="/" class="text-decoration-none" aria-label="Accueil">
                <i class="bi bi-house-door"></i>
            </a>
        </li>
        {% set breadcrumb = route_breadcrumb|default(null) %}
        {% if breadcrumb is not null %}
            {% for parent in breadcrumb.parents %}
                {% set parentMenuId = null %}
                {% for menu in menus|default([]) %}
                    {% if menu.label == parent %}
                        {% set parentMenuId = menu.id %}
                    {% endif %}
                {% endfor %}
                {% if parentMenuId is not null %}
                    <li class="breadcrumb-item">
                        <button type="button" class="btn btn-link p-0 border-0 align-baseline text-decoration-none breadcrumb-parent-btn" data-open-menu="{{ parentMenuId }}">{{ parent }}</button>
                    </li>
                {% else %}
                    <li class="breadcrumb-item text-body-secondary">{{ parent }}</li>
                {% endif %}
            {% endfor %}
            <li class="breadcrumb-item active text-truncate" aria-current="page">
                {{ breadcrumb_current|default(breadcrumb.label) }}
            </li>
        {% endif %}
    </ol>
</nav>
